<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <!--
      docs/plan/03 § Providers — the implementation assembly: "grains, reconcilers, the chart
      bindings". This one has a reconciler and nothing else: a widget has no grain of its own (see the
      .Contracts .csproj) and renders no chart (a ConfigMap is one object, and
      CyberCloud.Kubernetes.Charts does not exist).

      ⚠ ITS ENTIRE REFERENCE SET IS ITS OWN .Contracts. Everything else — IResourceReconciler,
      ReconcileContext, KubeCommand — arrives through it. docs/plan/07 § The enforcement seam:
      "Providers never call the engine. A provider that does is failing a review." Here that is
      mechanical rather than reviewed, because CyberCloud.ResourceManager.Contracts does not reference
      CyberCloud.Authorization.Contracts, so ICheckGrain is not nameable from this assembly at all.

      ⚠ NO Microsoft.Orleans.Server, NO Orleans.Multitenant AND NO KubernetesClient, deliberately. A
      reconciler is a plain singleton called by CyberCloud.ResourceManager's ReconcileDriver: it takes
      no grain references (so it needs no ForTenant and CC1006 has nothing to police here) and it
      names no k8s.Models type, which is docs/plan/03 § Assembly graph rules, rule 3. If a future
      provider needs Orleans in its implementation assembly, that is worth a second look before the
      package goes in.
    -->

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="../CyberCloud.Providers.Sample.Contracts/CyberCloud.Providers.Sample.Contracts.csproj"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup Label="CyberCloud.Analyzers — see CyberCloud.Core.csproj for why this is not a reference">
        <ProjectReference Include="../../../CyberCloud.Analyzers/CyberCloud.Analyzers.csproj"
                          OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                          ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"/>
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
